1
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:04,010
Take a key, and lay it on a flat grid.

2
00:00:04,680 --> 00:00:06,590
Now pick up the whole plane.

3
00:00:06,770 --> 00:00:08,230
Lean it to one side.

4
00:00:08,400 --> 00:00:09,400
Pull it upward.

5
00:00:09,510 --> 00:00:11,600
Lean it again, and once more.

6
00:00:12,280 --> 00:00:15,780
Four moves,
and together they make a single matrix.

7
00:00:16,450 --> 00:00:17,580
So here's the question.

8
00:00:17,730 --> 00:00:18,800
Can we put it back?

9
00:00:19,490 --> 00:00:20,300
We can.

10
00:00:20,340 --> 00:00:21,780
One more matrix,

11
00:00:21,820 --> 00:00:26,080
one smooth motion, and every point returns
to exactly where it started.

12
00:00:26,250 --> 00:00:28,860
That second matrix is called the inverse.

13
00:00:29,520 --> 00:00:32,100
Now try a matrix
that looks almost the same.

14
00:00:32,170 --> 00:00:34,070
Only its second column is different.

15
00:00:34,780 --> 00:00:36,240
The plane falls flat.

16
00:00:36,480 --> 00:00:39,360
The whole key is now a streak
on a single line.

17
00:00:40,040 --> 00:00:41,130
Put this one back.

18
00:00:41,690 --> 00:00:43,250
There's nothing to hold on to.

19
00:00:43,410 --> 00:00:45,620
This streak could have come from this key.

20
00:00:45,790 --> 00:00:47,560
Or this one, with a tooth flipped.

21
00:00:47,720 --> 00:00:50,510
Four teeth, each pointing up or down:

22
00:00:50,560 --> 00:00:55,270
that's sixteen different keys, and all
sixteen leave exactly the same mark.

23
00:00:55,890 --> 00:00:59,550
It's a shadow, and one shadow
can't tell you which key cast it.

24
00:01:00,220 --> 00:01:03,660
Why can one matrix be undone,
when its near twin can't?

25
00:01:03,810 --> 00:01:06,710
The answer decides
how a video game draws its world,

26
00:01:06,750 --> 00:01:10,487
how a drone reads a map,
how a computer solves an equation,

27
00:01:10,527 --> 00:01:13,110
and whether a blurred picture
can be made sharp again.

28
00:01:13,740 --> 00:01:16,880
And by the end,
we'll do something that sounds impossible.

29
00:01:17,050 --> 00:01:20,210
We'll rebuild this key
from nothing but its shadows.

30
00:01:21,860 --> 00:01:23,460
A matrix is a move.

31
00:01:23,560 --> 00:01:24,420
Its columns say

32
00:01:24,460 --> 00:01:27,930
where the two basic arrows land,
and the rest of the plane follows them.

33
00:01:28,590 --> 00:01:30,540
Some moves are easy to undo.

34
00:01:30,680 --> 00:01:34,720
Turn the plane, and the way back turns
it the same amount in the other direction.

35
00:01:34,850 --> 00:01:36,900
Stretch it, and the way back shrinks it.

36
00:01:37,050 --> 00:01:38,640
Slide each row sideways,

37
00:01:38,680 --> 00:01:42,710
like pushing a deck of cards, and the way
back pushes the deck the other way.

38
00:01:43,360 --> 00:01:45,540
That stretch suggests a shortcut.

39
00:01:45,700 --> 00:01:48,540
Two became a half,
and three became a third.

40
00:01:48,690 --> 00:01:51,370
So maybe you just flip
every number upside down.

41
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:54,050
But the sliding deck has a zero in it,

42
00:01:54,090 --> 00:01:58,870
and zero can't be flipped, yet we undid
that move by changing a single sign.

43
00:01:59,020 --> 00:02:02,300
And the matrix
that crushed our key has no zeros at all.

44
00:02:02,430 --> 00:02:05,370
So the numbers,
read one at a time, won't tell us.

45
00:02:05,480 --> 00:02:08,460
We have to watch what
the whole matrix does to space.

46
00:02:09,100 --> 00:02:11,287
Follow one point as the plane moves,

47
00:02:11,327 --> 00:02:14,100
and draw a thread from where it started
to where it landed.

48
00:02:14,220 --> 00:02:16,390
Now do the same for every other point.

49
00:02:17,020 --> 00:02:19,360
That web of threads is the move.

50
00:02:19,530 --> 00:02:23,120
The way back is the same web,
walked in the opposite direction.

51
00:02:23,230 --> 00:02:24,850
That's what an inverse is.

52
00:02:24,940 --> 00:02:26,690
Every thread, reversed.

53
00:02:27,320 --> 00:02:31,720
Go out along the threads and come back,
and every point ends up where it began.

54
00:02:31,870 --> 00:02:35,240
The round trip, taken as one move,
does nothing at all.

55
00:02:35,870 --> 00:02:38,010
Doing nothing has a matrix too.

56
00:02:38,050 --> 00:02:40,590
Its columns leave both arrows at home.

57
00:02:40,860 --> 00:02:44,190
We call it the identity,
and it behaves like the number one:

58
00:02:44,390 --> 00:02:46,680
multiply by it, and nothing changes.

59
00:02:47,450 --> 00:02:52,250
So the inverse is the matrix that, applied
after A, leaves you with the identity.

60
00:02:52,500 --> 00:02:53,873
Take the way back first,

61
00:02:53,913 --> 00:02:57,180
and the threads haven't changed,
so everyone still gets home.

62
00:02:57,440 --> 00:02:59,190
Either order gives the identity.

63
00:03:01,010 --> 00:03:02,760
Most moves come in stages.

64
00:03:02,800 --> 00:03:05,810
Say we lean the plane,
then give it a quarter turn.

65
00:03:05,980 --> 00:03:07,010
How do we get back?

66
00:03:07,860 --> 00:03:12,010
Undo the lean, then undo the turn,
and the grid doesn't come home.

67
00:03:12,210 --> 00:03:15,270
The turn happened last,
so it has to come off first.

68
00:03:15,480 --> 00:03:18,770
Undo the turn, then the lean,
and everything lines up.

69
00:03:19,240 --> 00:03:21,420
It works like a film played backward.

70
00:03:21,670 --> 00:03:25,790
The inverse of a chain is the inverse
of each link, in reverse order.

71
00:03:26,020 --> 00:03:30,120
So if a complicated move can be broken
into simple ones, we can rewind it,

72
00:03:30,160 --> 00:03:31,880
one simple move at a time.

73
00:03:33,580 --> 00:03:38,030
Let's do that for the matrix that bent
our key, using only slides and stretches.

74
00:03:38,710 --> 00:03:41,030
The first slide pulls one arrow upright.

75
00:03:41,270 --> 00:03:45,070
A second slide drops it onto the axis,
exactly where it belongs.

76
00:03:45,780 --> 00:03:48,553
The other arrow is standing
four times too tall,

77
00:03:48,593 --> 00:03:50,990
so we squash the plane
to a quarter of its height.

78
00:03:51,730 --> 00:03:53,480
One last slide straightens it.

79
00:03:53,700 --> 00:03:55,830
Four moves, and the key is home.

80
00:03:56,530 --> 00:03:59,480
But we never wrote the inverse down
as a single matrix.

81
00:03:59,530 --> 00:04:02,300
So let's play the same four moves
on a second grid,

82
00:04:02,380 --> 00:04:04,020
one that starts untouched.

83
00:04:04,200 --> 00:04:05,910
As the bent grid straightens out,

84
00:04:05,950 --> 00:04:09,320
the straight grid bends,
keeping a record of every step.

85
00:04:09,940 --> 00:04:14,120
When the first grid reaches the identity,
the second has become the inverse.

86
00:04:14,810 --> 00:04:16,330
If you've learned row reduction,

87
00:04:16,370 --> 00:04:19,930
those two blocks of numbers side
by side are these two grids.

88
00:04:20,030 --> 00:04:22,560
One rewinds, and the other records.

89
00:04:23,190 --> 00:04:24,160
Let's check it.

90
00:04:24,200 --> 00:04:26,253
Multiply the inverse by the original,

91
00:04:26,293 --> 00:04:30,890
and the fractions cancel, leaving ones
on the diagonal and zeros everywhere else.

92
00:04:31,570 --> 00:04:33,730
You may also recognize those four moves.

93
00:04:33,770 --> 00:04:37,430
They're the same four that bent
the key at the start, played backward.

94
00:04:38,110 --> 00:04:40,100
And there's something hiding in that list.

95
00:04:40,220 --> 00:04:42,350
A slide never changes area.

96
00:04:42,490 --> 00:04:45,390
So all of the shrinking came
from that single squash,

97
00:04:45,430 --> 00:04:46,570
down to a quarter.

98
00:04:47,200 --> 00:04:51,090
Four is exactly how much
the original matrix grew every area.

99
00:04:51,210 --> 00:04:52,370
It's the determinant.

100
00:04:53,090 --> 00:04:55,880
Run the same steps with letters
in place of numbers,

101
00:04:55,920 --> 00:04:58,880
and out comes the recipe
for any two by two matrix.

102
00:04:58,970 --> 00:05:01,267
Swap these two, negate those two,

103
00:05:01,307 --> 00:05:02,800
and divide by the determinant.

104
00:05:03,490 --> 00:05:05,600
Which should make you a little uneasy.

105
00:05:05,780 --> 00:05:08,010
What happens when there's nothing
to divide by?

106
00:05:09,490 --> 00:05:13,900
Take our matrix, and slowly tilt
its second column toward the first.

107
00:05:13,940 --> 00:05:19,300
The plane grows thinner, the determinant
falls, and the inverse has to work harder.

108
00:05:19,360 --> 00:05:23,960
To reopen a plane this thin,
it has to stretch by a factor of ten.

109
00:05:24,220 --> 00:05:25,460
Then a hundred.

110
00:05:25,590 --> 00:05:27,060
Then a thousand.

111
00:05:27,670 --> 00:05:30,140
Keep this nearly flat moment in mind.

112
00:05:30,190 --> 00:05:31,290
It's going to matter.

113
00:05:32,140 --> 00:05:35,400
When the two columns finally line up,
the plane is flat,

114
00:05:35,500 --> 00:05:39,380
the determinant is zero,
and the inverse has run off to infinity.

115
00:05:40,240 --> 00:05:43,730
Pick one spot on the line,
and ask which points landed there.

116
00:05:43,810 --> 00:05:47,010
The threads come
in from an entire line of points.

117
00:05:47,140 --> 00:05:49,550
These are the rays that cast our shadow.

118
00:05:50,420 --> 00:05:53,240
A way back would have to send
this one spot to all

119
00:05:53,280 --> 00:05:57,970
of those points at once, and a matrix
gives each input just one answer.

120
00:05:58,790 --> 00:06:00,470
You can read this in the columns.

121
00:06:00,510 --> 00:06:03,940
Here, the first column
is exactly twice the second,

122
00:06:03,980 --> 00:06:05,800
so the second adds nothing new.

123
00:06:05,840 --> 00:06:08,840
We say the columns are linearly dependent.

124
00:06:09,110 --> 00:06:11,960
And that relationship
is the crushed arrow itself:

125
00:06:12,120 --> 00:06:15,640
one of the first,
minus two of the second, makes nothing.

126
00:06:16,480 --> 00:06:19,670
So these are one fact,
seen from four sides.

127
00:06:19,710 --> 00:06:21,820
The columns depend on each other.

128
00:06:21,940 --> 00:06:23,690
The area drops to zero.

129
00:06:23,830 --> 00:06:25,780
A whole direction disappears.

130
00:06:25,820 --> 00:06:27,480
And there is no way back.

131
00:06:27,520 --> 00:06:30,480
If any one of these is true,
all of them are.

132
00:06:32,280 --> 00:06:35,830
Undoing a matrix is also what it means
to solve an equation.

133
00:06:36,350 --> 00:06:38,230
Say you're mixing two powders.

134
00:06:38,270 --> 00:06:42,120
A scoop of the first has three grams
of protein and two of fibre.

135
00:06:42,160 --> 00:06:45,660
A scoop of the second has one of protein
and two of fibre.

136
00:06:45,700 --> 00:06:48,100
Those are the columns of our matrix.

137
00:06:48,140 --> 00:06:50,910
Give it a number of scoops,
and it tells you what you get.

138
00:06:51,770 --> 00:06:54,150
Usually, though,
the question runs the other way.

139
00:06:54,190 --> 00:06:57,390
You need seven grams
of protein and six of fibre.

140
00:06:57,470 --> 00:06:59,390
How many scoops is that?

141
00:06:59,680 --> 00:07:03,320
Geometrically, that asks
which point landed on this target.

142
00:07:03,360 --> 00:07:05,100
And we know the way back.

143
00:07:05,240 --> 00:07:08,860
Apply the inverse, follow the thread home,
and there's the answer:

144
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:11,520
two scoops of the first,
one of the second.

145
00:07:12,310 --> 00:07:14,960
Every linear system asks the same thing.

146
00:07:15,000 --> 00:07:18,460
You know where something ended up,
and you want to know where it started.

147
00:07:19,260 --> 00:07:23,370
Now swap in a second powder that's
exactly half as strong as the first.

148
00:07:23,410 --> 00:07:26,430
Every mix you can make falls
on a single line.

149
00:07:26,480 --> 00:07:29,940
Ask for something off that line,
and no recipe exists.

150
00:07:29,980 --> 00:07:31,410
Ask for something on it,

151
00:07:31,450 --> 00:07:33,180
and there are endless recipes.

152
00:07:33,700 --> 00:07:35,540
No answer, or too many.

153
00:07:35,760 --> 00:07:40,500
Those are the two ways a system fails,
and both are the collapse we just saw.

154
00:07:42,110 --> 00:07:45,600
Two observers can describe the same place
in different ways.

155
00:07:45,640 --> 00:07:48,270
To a drone with its own tilted grid,

156
00:07:48,310 --> 00:07:51,420
this landmark is two steps ahead
and one to the side.

157
00:07:51,560 --> 00:07:55,920
The matrix built from the drone's
axes translates that onto the map,

158
00:07:55,960 --> 00:07:59,750
and its inverse translates the map
back into the drone's point of view.

159
00:08:00,640 --> 00:08:02,910
A video game does this for every frame.

160
00:08:02,950 --> 00:08:07,410
The camera is placed in the world
by a matrix, and to find out what it sees,

161
00:08:07,450 --> 00:08:10,740
the game applies the inverse
of that matrix to everything else.

162
00:08:10,780 --> 00:08:14,390
As far as the computer is concerned,
you never walk forward.

163
00:08:14,540 --> 00:08:17,250
The world gets carried backward, past you.

164
00:08:18,110 --> 00:08:21,050
Now here's a picture,
smeared by a shaking camera.

165
00:08:21,410 --> 00:08:24,040
Each blurred pixel is an average
of its neighbours,

166
00:08:24,080 --> 00:08:26,970
and averages are exactly
what a matrix computes.

167
00:08:27,010 --> 00:08:29,090
This blur has an inverse.

168
00:08:29,300 --> 00:08:31,630
Apply it, and every edge comes back.

169
00:08:32,100 --> 00:08:34,320
But remember that nearly flat plane.

170
00:08:34,720 --> 00:08:38,120
Blur squeezes the finest detail almost
to nothing,

171
00:08:38,160 --> 00:08:41,070
so the inverse has to stretch
it by an enormous amount.

172
00:08:41,120 --> 00:08:42,920
Add a trace of noise,

173
00:08:42,960 --> 00:08:47,170
too faint to see, and it gets stretched
too, until the picture is buried.

174
00:08:48,050 --> 00:08:52,390
So a matrix can have an inverse,
and that inverse can still be useless.

175
00:08:52,430 --> 00:08:57,020
Real deblurring stops short, and undoes
only the directions that survived.

176
00:08:58,900 --> 00:09:03,050
Which brings us back to the key,
and the shadow we couldn't undo.

177
00:09:03,380 --> 00:09:05,430
From one shadow, that's still true.

178
00:09:05,700 --> 00:09:07,920
But nothing stops us from taking another.

179
00:09:08,280 --> 00:09:09,153
Turn the light,

180
00:09:09,193 --> 00:09:13,420
and a tooth that could have pointed
either way now falls in a different place.

181
00:09:13,480 --> 00:09:18,540
Each new shadow adds new equations,
and fewer and fewer keys can fit them all.

182
00:09:19,350 --> 00:09:20,400
Two shadows.

183
00:09:20,500 --> 00:09:21,330
Four.

184
00:09:21,370 --> 00:09:22,380
Sixteen.

185
00:09:22,780 --> 00:09:25,240
A blur at first, then an outline.

186
00:09:25,410 --> 00:09:29,510
With enough angles, the combined
matrix no longer forgets anything,

187
00:09:29,700 --> 00:09:34,200
and here's the key, teeth and all,
rebuilt from shadows alone.

188
00:09:34,990 --> 00:09:38,000
This is how a CT scanner
sees inside your body.

189
00:09:38,040 --> 00:09:40,660
It records shadows
from hundreds of angles,

190
00:09:40,700 --> 00:09:42,860
then solves for what must have cast them.

191
00:09:43,670 --> 00:09:45,640
Every matrix moves space.

192
00:09:45,850 --> 00:09:48,280
The inverse asks what that move forgot.

193
00:09:48,890 --> 00:09:50,410
If it forgot nothing,

194
00:09:50,470 --> 00:09:53,670
every point still has a thread
leading back to where it began,

195
00:09:53,710 --> 00:09:55,440
and the way home exists.

196
00:09:55,490 --> 00:09:57,060
If it forgot something,

197
00:09:57,130 --> 00:10:00,680
that part is gone, and no amount
of algebra will bring it back.

198
00:10:00,940 --> 00:10:04,990
All you can do is go and look again,
from somewhere new.
